# Operating Model

This document defines the operating model for a tool-agnostic SDLC change governance framework in regulated finance, enterprise transformation, and high-control environments.

The model describes how governed change moves from intake through review, build, UAT, approval, release readiness, implementation, post-implementation review, and closure.

It is designed to improve traceability, accountability, evidence quality, release discipline, and decision reliability across business, technology, finance, risk, 
operations, and control stakeholders.

##

## Purpose

The operating model defines how change governance works in practice.

It clarifies:

- who participates in governed change
- what must be evidenced
- when control gates apply
- how lifecycle movement is controlled
- how risks and defects are surfaced
- how release readiness is assessed
- how implementation outcomes are reviewed
- how tools support, but do not replace, governance

The purpose is not to create bureaucracy. The purpose is to ensure that meaningful enterprise change is visible, reviewable, owned, tested, approved, and closed with appropriate evidence.

##

## Operating Principle

Governed change should not advance because work appears complete.

Governed change should advance because the required conditions for movement have been met.

Those conditions may include:

- clear business objective
- defined owner
- documented requirements
- risk classification
- testable acceptance criteria
- UAT evidence
- defect disposition
- approval record
- release-readiness confirmation
- implementation validation
- post-implementation review

The workflow tool records the movement. The operating model defines whether the movement is justified.

##

## Scope

This operating model applies to SDLC, process, data, reporting, control, and workflow changes that may affect regulated or high-control business environments.

Example change types include:

- finance reporting logic updates
- ledger or subledger process changes
- data quality remediation workflows
- reconciliation process changes
- operational control enhancements
- regulatory reporting readiness changes
- workflow automation changes
- dashboard or production reporting changes
- system configuration changes
- exception-management workflow changes
- access, entitlement, or role-based workflow changes
- post-implementation remediation items

##

## Operating Model Components

The model contains ten core components.

| Component | Purpose |
|---|---|
| Change Intake | Captures the business need, scope, ownership, and initial risk context |
| Requirements Review | Confirms that the change is sufficiently defined and testable |
| Risk Classification | Determines the governance depth required for the change |
| Control Gates | Defines evidence and approval conditions for lifecycle movement |
| Build / Configuration | Executes the approved technical, process, or data change |
| UAT Evidence | Demonstrates business validation against expected outcomes |
| Defect / Issue Disposition | Tracks failed tests, exceptions, accepted risks, or blocked items |
| Release Readiness | Confirms that implementation conditions have been met |
| Implementation Validation | Confirms the change was deployed or operationalized as expected |
| Post-Implementation Review | Reviews outcome, defects, lessons learned, and closure readiness |

